  quiz: [
    {q:"Kim holds a Utah producer license with the life line of authority only. Which product may she sell?", c:["A variable universal life policy","A fixed deferred annuity, once she completes the required annuity training","A variable annuity, if her insurer approves","A major medical policy"], a:1, w:"The life line includes nonvariable (fixed) annuities, but R590-230 requires a one-time 4-hour annuity course before any annuity sale. Variable products need the variable contracts line plus securities registration, and major medical needs the accident and health line.", o:"1.1", ut:true},
    {q:"Which combination is required to sell a variable annuity in Utah?", c:["Life line only","Accident and health line plus variable contracts line","Life line, variable contracts line, and FINRA securities registration","A consultant license"], a:2, w:"Utah grants the variable contracts line only to a producer who also holds the life line, and variable products are securities, so FINRA registration is also required. Life alone covers only fixed products.", o:"1.1", ut:true},
    {q:"Over his two-year period, a Utah life and health producer finished 30 CE hours: 3 of ethics, 14 classroom. He wants the extra 6 hours applied to his next period. What happens?", c:["The 6 hours roll over automatically","Up to 3 hours roll over","They roll over only if they were ethics hours","Excess hours don't carry over to the next licensing period"], a:3, w:"R590-142 says hours beyond those needed for renewal can't be carried over. He met the requirement (24 hours, 3 ethics, at least 12 classroom), but the extra 6 are simply lost.", o:"1.1", ut:true},
    {q:"Rosa received her life line of authority last month and wants to sell her first fixed indexed annuity. Under R590-230 she must first:", c:["Complete a one-time 4-credit-hour approved annuity training course","Pass the FINRA Series 6","Complete 24 hours of CE","Wait one year after licensing"], a:0, w:"A producer may not sell annuities until completing the one-time 4-credit annuity course, and the insurer must verify it. A fixed indexed annuity isn't a security, so no FINRA exam is needed.", o:"1.1", ut:true},
    {q:"A Utah producer is charged with a felony. When must he report it to the commissioner?", c:["Within 30 days after a conviction","Only at license renewal","Within 30 days of his initial court appearance","Within 10 days of the arrest"], a:2, w:"A criminal prosecution must be reported within 30 days of the initial appearance before a court. Waiting for a conviction is the classic wrong answer.", o:"1.1", ut:true},
    {q:"A producer tells a prospect, 'This participating whole life policy will pay you a $600 dividend every year.' This is:", c:["Permissible because dividends are guaranteed by a mutual insurer","A misleading communication, because future dividends can't be promised","Permissible if the insurer paid $600 last year","Rebating"], a:1, w:"Utah law treats assuring the payment of nonguaranteed future dividends in a specific amount as false or misleading information. Accurately reporting past dividend history is allowed, but promising a future amount is not. Rebating means giving part of the commission back.", o:"1.2", ut:true},
    {q:"An applicant answers 'yes' to the replacement question on a life application. Under Utah law, when must the producer present the Notice Regarding Replacement?", c:["Within 5 business days after the application","At policy delivery","Within 30 days after issue","No later than at the time the application is taken"], a:3, w:"31A-22-429 requires the producer to present the notice no later than when the application is taken. Sales material must be left by policy delivery, and the 5-business-day deadline belongs to the insurers.", o:"1.2", ut:true},
    {q:"In a life insurance replacement, the replacing insurer must do all of the following EXCEPT:", c:["Notify the existing insurer within 5 business days of receiving the application","Give the owner a 30-day right to return the new policy for a full refund","Keep copies of the replacement notice for at least 5 years","Get the existing insurer's written approval before issuing the new policy"], a:3, w:"R590-93-5 requires notifying the existing insurer, offering a 30-day free look, and keeping records at least 5 years. The existing insurer has no veto: it can only offer the owner information about the existing policy.", o:"1.2", ut:true},
    {q:"Which transaction is a replacement under R590-93?", c:["Buying a new policy and paying its premiums by borrowing against an existing policy's cash value","Converting group term to an individual policy with the same insurer under a filed program","Buying credit life on a car loan","Letting a nonrenewable term policy expire in 3 years"], a:0, w:"A financed purchase, using an existing policy's values to pay new premiums, is a replacement. Same-insurer conversions, credit life, and nonrenewable term ending within 5 years are all excluded from the rule.", o:"1.2", ut:true},
    {q:"A client refuses to give a producer information about her income, assets and liquidity needs, but still wants an annuity. Under R590-230 the producer:", c:["Must refuse to sell her any annuity","May recommend any annuity the insurer offers","May complete the sale without recommending it, and must get her signed statement documenting the refusal","Must report her to the Insurance Department"], a:2, w:"When a consumer refuses to provide profile information and the annuity isn't recommended, the producer's care obligation doesn't apply, but the refusal must be documented on a consumer-signed form. The rule doesn't bar the sale, and the insurer must still find the issue reasonable.", o:"1.2", ut:true},
    {q:"The 'best interest' standard in Utah's annuity rule means the producer:", c:["Becomes a fiduciary of the consumer","Must recommend the annuity with the lowest commission","Must monitor the annuity for the life of the contract","May not put the producer's or insurer's financial interest ahead of the consumer's when recommending an annuity"], a:3, w:"R590-230 requires the producer to act in the consumer's best interest without putting his or the insurer's financial interest first. It expressly does not create a fiduciary duty, require the lowest-cost product, or impose ongoing monitoring.", o:"1.2", ut:true},
    {q:"Max's Utah life insurer is liquidated. He owned one policy with a $750,000 death benefit and $260,000 of cash value, and he is still alive and has asked to surrender it. What is the most the Utah Life and Health Insurance Guaranty Association will pay toward the cash surrender?", c:["$200,000","$250,000","$260,000","$500,000"], a:0, w:"For a life policy, the association covers up to $200,000 of cash surrender value and up to $500,000 of death benefit, with a $500,000 cap per life. $250,000 is the annuity limit, a common mix-up.", o:"1.2", ut:true},
    {q:"A producer tells a nervous client, 'Don't worry about this company's rating. The state guaranty association backs every dollar.' This is:", c:["Allowed, because the statement is accurate","Prohibited, because no one may use the association's existence to sell insurance","Allowed if the producer gives the summary document","Allowed only for annuities"], a:1, w:"31A-28-119 bars insurers and producers from using the guaranty association for sales or inducement. The statement is also inaccurate, because coverage has limits. The summary document is delivered with the policy, but it doesn't make the sales pitch legal.", o:"1.2", ut:true},
    {q:"A Utah life insurer receives a policyholder's request to surrender a whole life policy for its cash value. Under R590-98, the insurer must normally pay within:", c:["20 days","30 days","60 days","6 months"], a:0, w:"Failing to honor a request for policy values within 20 days is an unfair practice. The statutory deferral of up to 6 months may be used only when the insurer's financial stability is at risk, and only after a filing with the commissioner.", o:"1.2", ut:true},
    {q:"Which federal law confirms that the states, not the federal government, are the primary regulators of insurance?", c:["Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act","Fair Credit Reporting Act","McCarran-Ferguson Act","USA PATRIOT Act"], a:2, w:"McCarran-Ferguson (1945) leaves insurance regulation to the states, with federal antitrust law still applying to boycott, coercion and intimidation. Gramm-Leach-Bliley allowed banks, insurers and securities firms to affiliate and set privacy rules.", o:"1.3", ut:false},
    {q:"An insurer's anti-money laundering program under the USA PATRIOT Act must cover all of these products EXCEPT:", c:["Individual whole life insurance","Individual deferred annuities","Individual universal life insurance","Individual 10-year term life insurance"], a:3, w:"Covered products are permanent life (not group), annuities (not group), and products with cash value or investment features. Term life has no cash value to move money through, so it isn't covered.", o:"1.3", ut:false},
    {q:"A life insurer orders an investigative consumer report on an applicant, based on interviews with his neighbors. Under the FCRA, the applicant must be:", c:["Notified only if the application is declined","Notified in writing within 3 days after the report is requested","Given the names of the people interviewed","Asked to waive his FCRA rights"], a:1, w:"An investigative report requires written notice within 3 days after it is requested, plus disclosure of its nature and scope on request. The adverse-action notice after a decline is a separate requirement and doesn't replace this one.", o:"1.3", ut:false},
    {q:"Under the federal privacy framework as Utah applies it, before an insurer shares an applicant's medical information with a nonaffiliated company for marketing, it needs:", c:["Only a privacy notice","An opt-out notice the consumer doesn't return","The consumer's authorization (opt-in)","Nothing, because HIPAA exempts insurers"], a:2, w:"Financial information can be shared with nonaffiliated parties unless the consumer opts out, but health information needs the consumer's affirmative authorization. HIPAA also protects health information held by health plans and requires authorization for most uses outside treatment, payment and operations.", o:"1.3", ut:true}
  ]
